{"title":"2026 AMC 8 Problems/Problem 21","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_21\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 21</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Markov_Chains)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Markov Chains)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Recursive)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Recursive)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Remark\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(Quick)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (Quick)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Thinking_Feet\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Thinking Feet</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_(Short)_Solution_by_Euclid_Spark_(ANIMATED!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video (Short) Solution by Euclid Spark (ANIMATED!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_in_Chinese_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution in Chinese by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_21\">Problem 21</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 21\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Charlotte the spider is walking along a web shaped like a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-pointed star, shown in the figure below. The web has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> outer points and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> inner points. Each time Charlotte reaches a point, she randomly chooses a neighboring point and moves to that point. Charlotte starts at one of the outer points and makes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> moves (re-visiting points is allowed). What is the probability she is now at one of the outer points of the star?\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2026_amc_8_q21.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/b1/2026_amc_8_q21.png/450px-2026_amc_8_q21.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"450\" height=\"434\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/b/b1/2026_amc_8_q21.png 1.5x\" /></a></span>\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/2/d62333dfe74e53c83ced5e9a3d42abab01835b42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } \\frac{1}{5}\\qquad\\textbf{(B) } \\frac{1}{4}\\qquad\\textbf{(C) } \\frac{2}{5}\\qquad\\textbf{(D) } \\frac{1}{2}\\qquad\\textbf{(E) } \\frac{3}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"375\" height=\"38\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The graph undergoes <b>rotational symmetry</b> meaning when scaled on a factor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />, here <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/b/94be282a752a4f13a21a13c8abbf7c6185434fa5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k = 5$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> of the points, it is the same as considering a fixed starting position of Charlotte. WLOG let this starting point be the topmost one. On the second turn, she cannot be on an outer point. Then she is forced to an inner point in a 1 chance. From any outer point, she must move to an outer point with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance. Finally, from that outer point, she must move to an inner point with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> chance. Therefore, the probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/8/1e81b1a1a18796748d2d7db13ab328aad20b10c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"26\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>In general, the probabilistic depends on if the pentagram is on some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />th moveset. For instance, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/9/3b9e1bd65a891136417e4324350aec4bab8f82ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_0 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"50\" height=\"15\" />, and all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/e/f6eae18025cc7ec0372e56c52511ba5149ad11df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_n = \\frac{1}{2}(1-p_{n-1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"138\" height=\"38\" />. The term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/6/1c6ec3fb374d42189707c4ef4a30d3b64a531eca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-p_{n-1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"82\" height=\"20\" /> is of the point that to get to an outer point one must come from an inner point. \n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p><p>Note to SquishBJ from Pinotation: You are absolutely correct, thank you for taking the time to review my solution. Constructive counting is a swing and miss here due to the fact the question doesn't ask for strict tournaments rather cases based on dependency. Good catch&#160;:&gt;\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We begin by noticing that from an outer point, the next move must be to an inner point as both adjacent points are inner. Next, there are four adjacent points - two are inner, two are outer. Thus, there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> probability for both. The final move depends on which type of point was the last one - if it was outer, the next one would have to be inner, so that gives us a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for an outer point. However, if it was inner, there is, again, a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> probability that the next one is outer. Therefore our final probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/e/b9e84477562b46e3dad0b6c6d86163dc6a3b9c4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0) = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } \\frac{1}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"282\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~OlympiadMaster\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Markov_Chains.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Markov_Chains)\">Solution 3 (Markov Chains)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Markov Chains)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that there are only two states due to five-fold symmetry. Hence, we can create a Markov Chain with two states: outer and inner points. Transitioning from an outer point to an inner point happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and transitioning from an inner point to an outer point happens with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. The spider always changes from states, and it never stays on the same state in two consecutive moves. The spider starts on an outer point, so the probability at Move 0 for outer points is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Enumerating the values for 3 moves using our recursive rules above:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/6/466e9c8c78ba13cb7c4db76f972604ac6b7a094b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|} \\hline &amp;&amp;\\\\ \\textbf{Move} &amp; \\textbf{Outer} &amp; \\textbf{Inner}\\\\ &amp;&amp;\\\\ \\hline &amp;&amp;\\\\ 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0\\\\ &amp;&amp;\\\\ \\hline &amp;&amp;\\\\ 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 1\\\\ &amp;&amp;\\\\ \\hline &amp;&amp;\\\\ 2 &amp; \\frac{1}{2} &amp; \\frac{1}{2}\\\\ &amp;&amp;\\\\ \\hline &amp;&amp;\\\\ 3 &amp; \\frac{1}{4} &amp; \\frac{3}{4}\\\\ &amp;&amp;\\\\ \\hline \\end{array}\\]\" width=\"190\" height=\"305\" />\nWe aim to find the probability of the spider being at an outer point on Move 3. Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff560281a05a337311f1a97552598d67e9c5fd07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)} \\frac{1}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"55\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~Reese's Pieces\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Recursive.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Recursive)\">Solution 4 (Recursive)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Recursive)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/1/df151678062aa7e93180c689cab0c21a2563e351.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"11\" /> be the probability that Charlotte is at an outer point after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> moves.\n</p><p>From the symmetry of the star graph we derive that the probabilities are:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Outer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8ea4e52bb41fa9c0a1f8a387ea39b5b9c993b74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\to$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> Inner with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /></li>\n<li>Inner <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8ea4e52bb41fa9c0a1f8a387ea39b5b9c993b74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\to$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> Outer with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Inner <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/e/b8ea4e52bb41fa9c0a1f8a387ea39b5b9c993b74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\to$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> Inner with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /></li></ul>\n<p>If Charlotte is at an outer point after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> moves, she must have been at an inner point after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> moves and then moved outward. Therefore\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e78147f99babac598f05b1e4af25efd8c1be8113.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p_{n+1}=\\frac12(1-p_n)\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>The initial condition is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/b/53b2492e1b0a83f28d8ca39e76515d6b2f018625.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p_0=1\\]\" width=\"50\" height=\"15\" />\nsince she starts at an outer point.\n</p><p>Rewrite the recurrence as\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/0/700eae3bfcf3e95fb8a8179bcd8eea499d07a640.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p_{n+1}=\\frac12-\\frac12 p_n.\\]\" width=\"130\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>First, we find the constant equilibrium solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/3/f635f1ae6c31e0c9fdf84302016cb72507b485df.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[c=\\frac12-\\frac12 c\\]\" width=\"87\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/7/b77de26bb4ebcd04333d9d063547bffcc3d82218.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac32 c=\\frac12\\]\" width=\"54\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/5/2f573f2f37098a4d38ddf9c4fdc124d02cadc8d6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[c=\\frac13.\\]\" width=\"48\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/8/338674de56ca0e8aba33be83e62f3a8e9b0fa351.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q_n=p_n-\\frac13.\\]\" width=\"98\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Substituting into the recurrence,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/7/2a71f3f40e7d351eb8c2679d9fc406951ae0a62c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p_{n+1}=\\frac12-\\frac12\\left(q_n+\\frac13\\right) =\\frac13-\\frac12 q_n.\\]\" width=\"283\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Thus\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/5/145074bad780c5700e572a859f5f316077ae3766.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q_{n+1}=-\\frac12 q_n.\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>We now have a clean equation.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/c/afc28a669e5401feb86a0a91a83c20ae54fdb054.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q_n=q_0\\left(-\\frac12\\right)^n.\\]\" width=\"128\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Now we do some simple algebra.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/b/c0b60e3a42be8debacba9ac7b50ba118e8103576.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q_0=p_0-\\frac13=1-\\frac13=\\frac23,\\]\" width=\"201\" height=\"37\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/c/e5c056002539510379a052a3d32fe642e1d9a5ca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[q_n=\\frac23\\left(-\\frac12\\right)^n.\\]\" width=\"126\" height=\"43\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/e/8ee1358e17fdeacc730418ebf2bd40c95a139a6a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p_n=q_n+\\frac13=\\frac13+\\frac23\\left(-\\frac12\\right)^n.\\]\" width=\"240\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>Now we plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/3/5635737307f5f0651cced8ee2e6558a426fd27b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=3$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c1ccd67c6e57905c16def68175d4e5b2abc77aa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[p_3=\\frac13+\\frac23\\left(-\\frac12\\right)^3 =\\frac13-\\frac1{12} =\\frac14.\\]\" width=\"276\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>Thus the probability Charlotte is at an outer point after <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> moves is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/6/716db9b835e309a2de68a3d349be4f279b46cd72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\frac14}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"26\" height=\"49\" />.\n</p><p>~JohnnyAppleseed\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark\">Remark</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Remark\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>This solution turns a recursive sequence into a geometric sequence. It's overkill for this question, but it could be used if we are finding the probability after a large number of moves, e.g., 2026 moves.\n</p><p>~TheRealMee\n~JohnnyAppleseed (Fixed minor grammatical errors and smoothed wording)\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28Quick.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(Quick)\">Solution 5 (Quick)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (Quick)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like solution 1 and 2 we know that we can choose one edge point to start on. From any edge point there are two possibilities to go to, we can just use one of these possibilities due to symmetry, making the problem easier to solve. Once we go to this point we can see that there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> probability that you go to an edge point and a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> probability that you stay in a center point. If you go to an edge point there is a 0 probability that you can end on an edge point. While if you stay in the middle, there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdc960c165b21ba43f7a90caa2893d40409d6944.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> probability that you go to an edge point on your 3rd move. We can calculate this using symmetry without loss of generality making this problem much easier to solve. Finally our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/7/0074cd2485cda0b22a05fd8fd7b42a351d8157c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12*\\frac12=\\frac14$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"82\" height=\"38\" /> which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/443bf04010ed003b2a64499dd4ec384bdf751725.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{B:\\frac14}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"56\" height=\"49\" />\n</p><p><br />\n(PS. Make sure to check whether you can use symmetry for a counting problem before you actually use it.)\n~SShreyas\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Thinking_Feet\">Video Solution by Thinking Feet</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Thinking Feet\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/WfXEbC4qGkM\">https://youtu.be/WfXEbC4qGkM</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YJnsl4zjdK8\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YJnsl4zjdK8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_.28Short.29_Solution_by_Euclid_Spark_.28ANIMATED.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_(Short)_Solution_by_Euclid_Spark_(ANIMATED!)\">Video (Short) Solution by Euclid Spark (ANIMATED!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video (Short) Solution by Euclid Spark (ANIMATED!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/8tsmSCPZ3K8\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/8tsmSCPZ3K8</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_.28AIME_Qualifier.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\">Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3GmtxMC-W1I&amp;t=2474s\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3GmtxMC-W1I&amp;t=2474s</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_in_Chinese_by_SquishBJ_.28AIME_Qualifier.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_in_Chinese_by_SquishBJ_(AIME_Qualifier)\">Video Solution in Chinese by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution in Chinese by SquishBJ (AIME Qualifier)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1fWu866EcU/?spm_id_from=333.1387.upload.video_card.click\">https://www.bilibili.com/video/BV1fWu866EcU/?spm_id_from=333.1387.upload.video_card.click</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AMC_8_Problems/Problem_21&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\